<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Carafa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Carafa was a powerful noble family of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, holding many fiefs and princely titles from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its most famous member, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Gian Pietro Carafa</a>, became Pope Paul IV (1555–1559), a stern leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> whose nephews were disgraced after his death. Through marriage, the Carafa princes of Stigliano inherited the small Duchy of Sabbioneta after 1591.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-carafa.jpg" alt="House of Carafa" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sanngreal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carafa_stemma_barocco.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
